%S A398819 1,2,5,6,7,10,13,14,21,22,23,26,29,30,33,34,37,38,39,40,41,42,46,53,
%T A398819 54,55,56,57,58,61,64,65,66,69,70,73,77,78,82,85,86,87,88,93,94,101,
%U A398819 102,103,104,105,106,109,110,113,114,118,119,122,129,130,133,134,135
%N A398819 Numbers k such that the powerful parts (A057521) of both k and k+1 are cubes.
%C A398819 Numbers k such that every exponent in the prime factorization of both k and k+1 is either 1 or a multiple of 3.
%C A398819 Numbers k such that k and k+1 are both terms in A398818.
%C A398819 First differs from A372690 at n = 31: a(31) = 64 is not a term in A372690. Also A372690(58) = 127 is the least term in A372690 that is not a term in this sequence.
%C A398819 First differs from A391397 at n = 23: A391397(23) = 44 is not a term in this sequence. Also a(31) = 64 is the least term in this sequence that is not a term in A391397.
%C A398819 The asymptotic density of this sequence is d = zeta(3) * Product_{p prime} (1 - 2/p^2 + 1/p^3 - 2/p^4 + 2/p^5) = 0.452243874844828598751... . The number of terms not exceeding x is d * x + O(x^(2/3+eps)), where eps > 0 (Srichan et al., 2026, a consequence of Theorem 1.1).
%H A398819 Amiram Eldar, <a href="/A398819/b398819.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A398819 Teerapat Srichan, Nithi Rungtanapirom, and Saeree Wananiyakul, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1051/ita/2026022">On the distribution of consecutive M-free integers</a>, RAIRO - Theoretical Informatics and Applications, Vol. 60 (2026), Article 22.
%e A398819 23 is a term since it is squarefree, and thus its powerful part is 1, which is a cube, and the powerful part of 23 + 1 = 24 = 2^3 * 3 is 8 = 2^3, which is also a cube.
%t A398819 q[k_] := q[k] = AllTrue[FactorInteger[k][[;;, 2]], # == 1 || Divisible[#, 3] &]; Select[Range[135], q[#] && q[#+1] &]
%o A398819 (PARI) is1(k) = #select(x -> (x > 1 && x%3), factor(k)[,2]) == 0;
%o A398819 isok(k) = is1(k) && is1(k+1);
%Y A398819 Subsequence of A398818.
%Y A398819 A007674 is a subsequence.
%Y A398819 Cf. A002117, A057521, A372690, A391397.
